media: i2c: dw9719: add back the i2c device id table

Upstream v6.19 dropped the i2c_device_id table from this driver (and
.id_table with it) while converting it to CCI. That breaks every ACPI
platform, because there the CIO2 bridge instantiates the VCM as an i2c
client carrying a *software* node, not an OF node:

    i2c-INT347A:00-VCM   modalias = i2c:dw9719

With no of_node the OF table can never match, so the VCM never probes.
ov8865 then sits forever in v4l2-async waiting for its fwnode provider,
the CIO2 notifier never completes, no media links get created, and
libcamera's registerCameras() returns -ENODEV - i.e. *no* cameras appear
at all. The front ov5693 is a collateral victim: it has no VCM of its
own, it is simply behind the same notifier.

This restores what v6.18 had.

Applies to : linux v6.19.x, drivers/media/i2c/dw9719.c
             (assumes the fsleep() hunk of 0002 is already present, which
             it is on any linux-surface kernel)
Apply with : patch -p1 < 0001-...patch     (run from the kernel tree root)

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/dw9719.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/dw9719.c
@@ -451,6 +451,18 @@
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, dw9719_of_table);
 
+/*
+ * On ACPI systems the bridge instantiates the VCM as an i2c client carrying a
+ * software node, so it has no of_node and the OF table above can never match
+ * it. Keep an i2c id table for those platforms.
+ */
+static const struct i2c_device_id dw9719_id_table[] = {
+	{ "dw9719", DW9719 },
+	{ "dw9761", DW9761 },
+	{ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(i2c, dw9719_id_table);
+
 static DEFINE_RUNTIME_DEV_PM_OPS(dw9719_pm_ops, dw9719_suspend, dw9719_resume,
 				 NULL);
 
@@ -462,6 +474,7 @@
 	},
 	.probe = dw9719_probe,
 	.remove = dw9719_remove,
+	.id_table = dw9719_id_table,
 };
 module_i2c_driver(dw9719_i2c_driver);
 
